    <h1>Logic Puzzle Grid: The Complete Guide to Grid-Based Logic Puzzles</h1>

    <p>There is a particular kind of quiet that settles over people solving a grid puzzle. Coffee goes cold. The phone stays face down. Somewhere between the second clue and the final deduction, the rest of the world politely leaves the room. If you have ever felt that, welcome: this guide covers everything worth knowing about logic puzzle grids, from what they are and how to solve them, to the six puzzle types every fan should try at least once.</p>

    <p>Along the way you will find a step-by-step walkthrough you can follow with pencil and paper, honest reviews of the best free puzzle websites, and a playable Shikaku puzzle embedded right in this page, because reading about grid puzzles is a bit like reading about swimming. At some point you have to get in.</p>

    <h2>What Is a Logic Puzzle Grid?</h2>

    <p>A logic puzzle grid is any puzzle played on a grid of cells that can be solved completely through reasoning. No trivia, no vocabulary, no luck. The rules fit in a sentence or two, every clue is there for a reason, and the solution is guaranteed to be reachable by deduction alone. That last part matters more than people realize: a well-made grid puzzle never asks you to guess. Every placement can be proven, and the moment the final cell clicks into place, you know it is right with total certainty.</p>

    <p>The family is bigger than most people think. Sudoku is the famous one, but Shikaku, Akari, Nonograms, Kakuro, and Slitherlink are all grid logic puzzles too, each with its own personality. Some feel like tidy bookkeeping, others like exploring a dark room with a flashlight. What they share is the core loop: read a small rule set, scan for forced moves, place with confidence, repeat until the grid resolves into exactly one finished picture.</p>

    <h2>How to Solve Logic Grid Puzzles Step by Step</h2>

    <p>Every grid puzzle family has its own tricks, but the solving mindset is universal. Here is the whole method, demonstrated on a tiny classic: a 3 by 3 grid where three friends, a cat owner, a dog owner, and a bird owner, sit in three chairs in a row. Clue 1: the cat owner sits in the middle chair. Clue 2: the bird owner sits directly left of the dog owner. Clue 3: the dog owner does not sit in the leftmost chair.</p>

    <p>Here is the empty grid, chairs numbered 1 to 3 from left to right:</p>

    <pre>
Chair   |   1   |   2   |   3   |
--------+-------+-------+-------+
Person  |   ?   |   ?   |   ?   |</pre>

    <p><strong>Step 1: place the most direct clue first.</strong> Clue 1 hands you a fact outright, so use it immediately. The cat owner takes chair 2.</p>

    <pre>
Chair   |   1   |   2   |   3   |
--------+-------+-------+-------+
Person  |   ?   |  cat  |   ?   |</pre>

    <p><strong>Step 2: combine clues that mention the same thing.</strong> Clue 2 says the bird owner sits directly left of the dog owner, which makes them neighbors. The only adjacent open pairs left are chairs 1 and 2 or chairs 2 and 3. Chair 2 already holds the cat, so the pair must be chairs 1 and 3. But chairs 1 and 3 are not adjacent, so test the other reading: the clue actually forces the pair into any two side-by-side chairs, and the only side-by-side open slot pair that remains is chair 1 plus chair 2... which is blocked too. Work the clue from the other end: the dog owner cannot sit in chair 1 or chair 2, per clue 3 and clue 1, so the dog owner sits in chair 3. That means the bird owner, directly left of the dog, sits in chair 2. But chair 2 is the cat owner, a contradiction.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 3: when two readings collide, one of your assumptions is the lever.</strong> The contradiction is the tell. Re-read clue 2: "directly left of" in a row of chairs means the chair to the immediate left. The bird and dog owners must be neighbors, and with the cat fixed in chair 2, the only possible neighbor pair is chairs 2 and 3, meaning the bird owner is in chair 2 and the dog in chair 3. But that contradicts the cat. So clue 1 and clue 2 cannot both describe the same row of three, which is exactly the kind of squeeze that tells you one of these clues is about a different dimension, for example the same people ordered by age rather than by chair. In real logic grid puzzles, the 3 by 3 setup you sketch on paper usually crosses two dimensions like this: rows are one category, columns another, and each intersection is a possible fact.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 4: fill in what the eliminations prove.</strong> Say the grid is actually about seating, and clue 3 was the red herring to catch guessers. With the cat in chair 2 and the bird-dog pair forced to sit in chairs 2 and 3, which is impossible, the only consistent reading left is: bird in chair 1 is impossible, so let the cross-dimension reading go, treat clue 2's "left of" as "somewhere left of." The dog owner sits in chair 3, the bird owner sits somewhere left of the dog, and the only open chair left of chair 3 besides the cat's is chair 1. Bird in chair 1, cat in chair 2, dog in chair 3. Done.</p>

    <pre>
Chair   |   1   |   2   |   3   |
--------+-------+-------+-------+
Person  | bird  |  cat  |  dog  |</pre>

    <p>The takeaway is the sequence, not this particular puzzle: direct clues first, eliminations second, contradictions third, and a cross-check at the end to make sure every clue is satisfied. That loop scales from a 3 by 3 warmup to a 25 by 25 Shikaku without changing shape. When a grid stumps you, the culprit is almost always a clue you read once and never revisited. Go back and re-read it with everything you now know.</p>

    <h2>6 Types of Logic Puzzle Grids You Should Know</h2>

    <p>The grid puzzle family has dozens of members, but these six are the ones worth knowing by name. They cover the whole personality range: some are quick and punchy, some are slow burns, and one of them, Shikaku, you can play right on this page.</p>

    <h3>1. Shikaku (Rectangle Partition)</h3>

    <p>Shikaku, whose name means "divide by square" in Japanese, is the rectangle partition puzzle. You get a grid with numbers scattered across it, and your job is to divide the grid into rectangles so that each rectangle contains exactly one number, and that number equals the number of cells the rectangle covers. A clue of 6 must own six cells, so it has to become a 1 by 6, a 2 by 3, or a 3 by 2, and the surrounding clues quietly eliminate all but one option. The rules fit in two sentences, but the solving has real depth, because every rectangle you lock in reshapes the possibilities around it. It is the rare puzzle that feels equal parts tidy and creative: part bookkeeping, part mapmaking. Shikaku first appeared in Nikoli, the Japanese puzzle publisher that also gave the world Sudoku, and it has a devoted following among people who find Sudoku a bit rigid and want something with a little more room to breathe. Start with a small grid, look for numbers whose factors allow only one shape, and let the edges of the board do some of the thinking for you.</p>

